[about]
Title=3 - Open a Folder and Find a File
Intro=A folder as a list: arrowing it, jumping to a file by name with Jump, and opening what you find. This walk assumes walks two to three.
Setup=FileDir is open on a folder of saved web pages.
Homework=Open a folder of your own, jump to a file by its first letters, and open it.

[step]

Say=Every folder opens as a list you arrow through. the reader says the folder and how many items are in it.
Key=
Hear=FileDir
Hear=List box C:\PodcastDirectories
Hear=119 items
Note=The count comes once, when the folder opens.

[step]

Say=Up and Down Arrows move through the list; a letter jumps to the first name starting with it. Enter on a folder goes in; Backspace comes back up.
Key=DownArrow
Hear=Blind Movers dot h t m

[step]

Say=Control plus J jumps to a file by name. FileDir asks for the text and remembers your last ten answers.
Key=Control+J
Hear=Jump
Hear=Text edit combo
Note=The window is named Jump because the key is J.

[step]

Say=Type access and press Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=Access On dot h t m
Note=Jump looks at names. Control plus K looks inside the files.

[step]

Say=Control plus G, Go To, takes a path, completing it as you type, or offers your recent folders from a list.
Key=Control+G
Hear=Go To dialog, Folder: edit combo

[step]

Say=Escape. Alt plus R lists the folders opened this session, newest first; one Enter returns to any of them.
Key=Escape
Hear=FileDir, List box

[step]

Say=Shift plus I, Initial Change, jumps to the next name starting with a different letter -- a fast way down a long folder; Shift plus X, Extension Change, jumps to the next kind of file.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus F filters the folder to the files matching a pattern -- star dot m p 3, or two patterns with a bar between -- until it is cleared; the status says filtered.
Key=Control+F
Hear=Filter dialog, Pattern: edit combo

[step]

Say=Escape. Control plus K, Keywords, finds a word inside the body of files, not in their names; walk five uses it on a page of episodes.
Key=Escape
Hear=FileDir, List box

[step]

Say=Shift plus Q adds the current file or folder to the Quick folder, your list of favorites; Control plus Q opens that folder, from anywhere, and Enter there goes straight to the item.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Opening what you find. Enter opens the file in the program Windows has for it; Control plus E opens it in your editor, EdSharp when it is installed; Control plus W in your word processor; Shift plus Enter opens it as text whatever it is, which is how a strange file is looked into.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The question mark, What Content, says what is inside the current file without opening it: its kind, its size, its dates, the number of lines in a text file.
Key=Shift+Slash
Hear=Plain text, 12 kilobytes, 212 lines, changed 3 October 2026

[step]

Say=Control plus Shift plus T, Type Extended, says more: every property of the current item in one alphabetical list, such as a document's title and author, or a sound file's length. Walk five shows the Player's own Extra Info.
Key=Control+Shift+T
Hear=Type Extended, read only edit
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Escape. Shift plus Apostrophe says the folder you are in with its path, and the Apostrophe alone the current line with its tag, for when you have been away.
Key=Escape
Hear=FileDir, List box

[step]

Say=A planned misstep. Type a letter meaning to jump and nothing moves, because no name starts with it; the reader says the same line again. Control plus J with a word inside the name finds it instead.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The way back: Alt plus Left Arrow returns to the folder you came from, as a browser's Back does; Alt plus Right Arrow goes forward again. Together with Recent Folders, Alt plus R, you are never lost.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A path can be typed anywhere a folder is asked for, with the drive, and FileDir completes it as you type; a network share works the same way, with its two backslashes.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I say the key; the reader says what it does.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus J.
Key=
Hear=Jump
